course: dictionary
lesson: 12505
title: Things running out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Depletion is a noun. It names something getting used up, so there is much less of it left. «The town worried about the depletion of its water supply during the long dry summer.» The water supply is running low, so depletion means the amount is going down.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like «running low», «less and less» or «used up» often sit near it. Try swapping in "using up": «the using up of its water supply» still makes sense, so depletion means using up.
explain: Depletion is not only about nature. «The phone battery showed steady depletion all afternoon» means the charge kept going down. The key idea is that the amount keeps getting smaller, and it may not come back easily.
fact: Depletion is a noun, so it names a thing or a process.
fact: «Depletion» means something being used up, so much less is left.
fact: «Depletion» is often used in science and geography, for things like water, soil goodness, fish and the ozone layer.
fact: In everyday life, depletion can mean energy or money going down.
fact: Depletion usually has no plural, so we do not say «depletions» when we mean the general idea.
exercise: «The depletion of fish stocks worried the fishing boats.» What does depletion mean here? | Boats being painted bright colours | Fish swimming happily in a big group | *Fish being used up until there are far fewer left | Fish being cooked for dinner
exercise: «The river dried up after years of water depletion.» What is happening to the water? | *It is being used up, so much less is left | It is flowing faster than ever | It is being poured into bottles | It is getting warmer and boiling
exercise: Dad says, «The phone battery shows steady depletion all day.» What does he mean? | *The charge keeps going down | The phone has a new case | The battery is full and shiny | The phone is ringing loudly
test: What is the best meaning of depletion? | Something growing bigger every year | Something being hidden away | Something being cleaned carefully | *Something being used up, so much less is left
test: The lesson says depletion may not come back easily. What does that tell you? | The amount is free for everyone | The amount is growing fast | The amount is kept safe in a box | *The amount keeps getting smaller and is hard to restore
exam: Which sentence uses depletion correctly? | *The steady depletion of the forest left fewer trees each year | The depletion party was held under the trees | The forest had a bright depletion of colourful flowers | Depletions of the forest were painted red
